foreign ministers of g7 countries  including canada forming a united front  against russia the focus of talks at a  gathering in liverpool england have  largely focused on the ramped up  military presence of russian troops on  its border with ukraine we've been clear  that any incursion by russia into  ukraine would have massive consequences  for which there would be a severe cost  saturday u.s president joe biden who  spoke with russia's vladimir putin  earlier this week was even more direct  i've made it absolutely clear president  putin that  if he moves on ukraine  the economic consequences for his  economy are going to be devastating  devastating  a ramping up of rhetoric is being felt  across europe with the vatican calling  for calm  pope francis prayed for ukraine asking  that tensions be resolved through  serious international dialogue and not  with weapons moscow's latest buildup of  troops and military equipment is along  the border of eastern ukraine near an  area largely controlled by  russian-backed separatists the  cross-border tensions are a reminder of  what happened in 2014 when putin annexed  crimea  at that time western nations imposed  crippling sanctions on russia and  they're threatening to do the same again  but observers say one big difference is  western nations weren't taken by  surprise this time so we shouldn't  expect a matching of military might the  message here is we will not fight you  but we will  raise the cost of any further aggression  but statement points out putin is  unpredictable and at this point his end  goal is unclear  he's not a chess master  he's a judo artist and that means that  he just looks for individual  opportunities to strike not really  thinking five steps ahead and what  nato's and the g7 are doing is trying to  signal that this one step is too costly  on its own so now the world waits to see  if putin heeds that warning  mike lacouter global news ottawa
 

